* Basic Bash Commands

### 1. `pwd` – Print Working Directory
Displays the current directory path.

* `pwd -L` – Displays the logical path including symbolic links
* `pwd -P` – Displays the physical path resolves symbolic links
* `pwd --help` – Displays help information

---

### 2. `ls` – List Directory Contents
Lists files and directories in the current working directory.

* `ls -l` – Displays detailed file information permissions size owner date
* `ls -a` – Displays all files including hidden files starting with `.`
* `ls -lt` – Displays detailed information sorted by modification time

---

### 3. `cd` – Change Directory
Navigates between different directories.

* `cd ..` – Moves up one level to the parent directory
* `cd [directory_name]` – Changes directory to the specified path
* `cd` or `cd ~` – Navigates directly to the user home directory

---

### 4. `mkdir` – Make Directory
Creates new directories.

* `mkdir [directory_name]` – Creates a single new directory
* `mkdir -v [directory_name]` – Creates a directory and prints a message for each created directory
* `mkdir -p [path/to/directory]` – Creates parent directories as needed if they do not exist
* `mkdir -m [mode] [directory_name]` – Sets file permission bits mode upon creation

---

### 5. `rmdir` – Remove Empty Directory
Removes empty directories from the filesystem.

* `rmdir [directory_name]` – Removes an empty directory
* `rmdir -v [directory_name]` – Displays a message for every directory processed
* `rmdir -p [path]` – Removes the directory and its parent directories if they become empty

---

### 6. `rm` – Remove Files and Directories
Deletes files or directories.

* `rm [file_name]` – Deletes a specified file
* `rm -i [file_name]` – Prompts for confirmation before deleting each file
* `rm -r [directory_name]` – Recursively deletes a directory and all of its contents
* `rm -f [file_name]` – Forces deletion without prompting or showing errors for non-existent files
* `rm -rf [directory_name]` – Forcefully and recursively removes a directory and all its contents

---

### 7. `touch` – Create File / Update Timestamp
Creates empty files or modifies existing file timestamps.

* `touch [file_name]` – Creates a new empty file if it does not exist
* `touch -a [file_name]` – Updates only the access time
* `touch -m [file_name]` – Updates only the modification time

---

### 8. `cp` – Copy Files and Directories
Copies files or directories from one location to another.

* `cp [source] [destination]` – Copies a file to a specified destination
* `cp -i [source] [destination]` – Prompts before overwriting an existing destination file
* `cp -v [source] [destination]` – Displays verbose details while copying
* `cp -r [source_dir] [dest_dir]` – Recursively copies a directory and its contents

---

### 9. `mv` – Move / Rename Files
Moves or renames files and directories.

* `mv [old_name] [new_name]` – Renames a file or directory
* `mv [file_name] [destination_dir]` – Moves a file into a target directory
* `mv -i [source] [destination]` – Prompts before overwriting an existing file
* `mv -v [source] [destination]` – Displays verbose output during the operation

---

### 10. `head` – Output Beginning of Files
Prints the first part of files.

* `head [file_name]` – Displays the first 10 lines of a file by default
* `head -n [number] [file_name]` – Displays the specified number of initial lines

---

### 11. `tail` – Output Ending of Files
Prints the last part of files.

* `tail [file_name]` – Displays the last 10 lines of a file by default
* `tail -n [number] [file_name]` – Displays the specified number of trailing lines
* `tail -f [file_name]` – Monitors and streams real-time updates as the file grows
